The U.S. Supreme Court is often presented as a circle of nine justices, as if the Constitution had so willed it. The number appears nowhere in it. Article III vests the judicial power in “one Supreme Court” and in the lower courts that Congress establishes, without saying how many judges sit on them. The Court's own website says as much: the Constitution leaves it to Congress to set their number.

Congress exercised that power from day one. The judiciary act of September 24, 1789, the Judiciary Act, provides for a Court made up of “a chief justice and five associate justices,” with four justices needed for a quorum. Six justices, then, for a Court whose members also sit out in the country: the same law organizes circuit courts, made up of two Supreme Court justices and the district judge.

## A Number That Follows the Map of the Circuits

The list kept by the Federal Judicial Center, the research arm of the U.S. federal courts, shows that the number never stopped moving:

- 1801: a law provides for reducing the Court to five seats at the first vacancy, but no vacancy occurs before its repeal;
- 1802: the repeal restores the six seats;
- 1807: a seventh seat is created;
- 1837: two more seats, making nine;
- 1863: a tenth seat;
- 1866: the law reduces the Court to seven seats and forbids filling vacancies until that number is reached. The number of sitting justices falls to eight before the next revision;
- 1869: the law sets nine justices, one for each of the judicial circuits created in 1866.

Of these eight laws, only five actually changed the number of seats: 1807, 1837, 1863, 1866, 1869. The Court had nine seats in 1837, ten in 1863, seven in 1866, then nine again in 1869.

## What This Shifts

The nine of 1869 thus has a date and, in the law itself, an administrative reference point: one justice per circuit. The law does not present it as the right size for a court. From 1789 on, Supreme Court justices sit in the circuit courts, and in 1869 their number is pegged to that of the circuits. No constitutional text makes it a sacred number.

The finding is a legal one: what one law had set, another law could change, and the Congresses of 1807, 1837, 1863, and 1866 did so. What remains to be understood is why a number set by an ordinary law so readily passes for an article of the Constitution.
